cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use these tools, please cite them. This citation covers the tools only, not the
  models. If you use one of the converted models published on Hugging Face
  (https://huggingface.co/collections/Valen-Peregrine/faunapulse), please also cite its
  original authors, as given on its model card.
title: "Tools to convert and quantise detection and identification models for the FaunaPulse app"
abstract: >-
  Python tools that convert animal detection and identification models into LiteRT files
  (.tflite, formerly TensorFlow Lite) for FaunaPulse, an Android app that detects, tracks and
  identifies animals on the phone itself. They convert Ultralytics YOLO detectors
  (segmentation models such as flat-bug are turned into box detectors), the insectDCT
  hierarchical classifier and the image encoders of BioCLIP 1, 2 and 2.5. They can make the
  files smaller by quantisation (fp16 or 8-bit weights; for detectors also full INT8, which
  needs calibration images) and check how closely a converted file's outputs match those of
  the original PyTorch model. They also build label packs for BioCLIP: the lists of candidate
  taxa it chooses from on the phone, with names from the BioCLIP authors' TreeOfLife-200M
  list, for example the main pollinator orders of each continent or the mammals and birds of
  the world. A pack can also hold a list of names for each country, made from GBIF occurrence
  records, so that the app can narrow the candidates to one country. A test script tries the
  packs on iNaturalist photos uploaded after the model's training data, and an upload script
  publishes the converted files on Hugging Face, each with a model card. The tools also work
  for other models of the same kinds, for example YOLO detectors that users train themselves.
  The models published so far were trained by others: each model card names their original
  authors and how to cite them. The conversions are unofficial, not provided or endorsed by
  the original authors. This repository holds the code and the texts of the model cards; the
  model files themselves are on Hugging Face.
